  "text_md": "10 V. ASATRYAN, E. BABASYAN, AND S. MKRTCHYAN\n\nFrom now on we will drop the subscript from the notation C≤k,w. For a sequence s denote by σ(s) and d(s) respectively its Schnirelmann density and\n\nasymptotic density. Namely,\n\nA(n) n\n\nσ(s) = inf\n\n, and\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\nn\n\nA(n) n\n\nd(s) = lim\n\nif it exists, where A(n) = |{i : si ≤ n}|.\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nn→∞\n\nRecall Mann’s theorem, relating sumsets to Schnirelmann densities:\n\nTheorem 3.4 (Mann’s Theorem [Man42]). For subsets A and B of N, if the sumset A + B = N, then\n\nσ(A + B) ≥ σ(A) + σ(B) holds.\n\nWe now use Mann’s theorem together with the results above to establish Theorem 1.4.\n\nProof of Theorem 1.4. By Lemma 3.3 it is enough to prove P(C≤k,w) = 1. By Lemma 3.1 Ck,w is a tail event, and by Lemma 3.3 we have P(C≤k,w) = P(Ck,w), so it is enough to prove that P(C≤k,w) > 0. From EX < k if follows that d({Wn}n∈N) > k1. Introduce the random variable N by\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\nN = min n : ∀m ≥ n,\n\nA(m) m\n\n1 k\n\n>\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile5.png>)\n\n.\n\nSince ∞n=1 P(N = n) = 1, it follows that ∃n0 such that P(N = n0) > 0. We now distinguish two cases. Case 1: 1 ∈ SuppX. We have\n\nP (N = n0 for the sequence generated by {Xi}i>n\n\n) > 0, and since 1 ∈ SuppX, we have\n\n0\n\nP(X1 = X2 = · · · = Xn\n\n= 1) > 0. Since these two events deal with distinct parts of the i.i.d. sequence {Xi}i>n\n\n0\n\n, with positive probability both events occur simultaneously. Now, let us evaluate the Schnirelmann density σ({Wi}i≥1) for such sequences of gaps. We have\n\n0\n\n \n\n1 if n ≤ n0\n\nA(n) n\n\n- 1\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile6.png>)\n\n- 2 if n0 < n ≤ 2n0\n\n\n≥\n\n,\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile7.png>)\n\n1\n\n\n\nk if n > 2n0\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile8.png>)\n\nwhere the ﬁrst two inequalities follow from the choice of the ﬁrst n0 gaps and the last one from the condition N = n0.\n\nThus we get P σ(W) ≥ k1 > 0 and Mann’s theorem implies asymptotic ≤ k-completeness with the same positive probability.\n\n![](<2503.05177_pg10_images/imageFile9.png>)\n\nCase 2: 1 ∈/ SuppX.\n\n",
